# =============================================================================
# FALCONAge - build context exclusions
# =============================================================================
# Dockerfile.cpu ends with `COPY . /opt/falconage/src`. Without this file that
# COPY drags in a working tree of well over a gigabyte -- upstream clones, notes,
# whatever the corpus fetcher has already downloaded -- sent to the daemon on
# every build and cached in every layer, for files no image reads.
#
# Docker applies these top to bottom and the last matching rule wins, so the
# `!` re-admissions must follow their exclusions.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---- the repository root is allow-list only ----------------------------------
# Same reasoning as .gitignore. `*` here matches every root entry, and only the
# directories an image actually reads are re-admitted. A deny-list of working
# material fails silently the first time something new appears in the root; this
# fails by leaving a needed file out of the build, which the build then says so
# about.
#
# Note that Docker's `*` -- unlike git's `/*` -- excludes root entries only
# because there is no leading slash to anchor; the per-directory rules below
# still apply inside what is re-admitted.
*
!colorscheme.yaml
!docker/
!python/
!r/
!test/

# ---- version control ---------------------------------------------------------
.git
.gitignore
.github
.gitattributes

# ---- test corpus -------------------------------------------------------------
# The payloads are mounted at run time (-v ./test/data:/data), never baked in:
# a public dataset inside an image is a redistribution nobody asked for, and it
# would put the image over 1 GB for data that changes independently of the code.
#
# The manifest and the two fetchers ARE copied -- Dockerfile.testdata installs
# them at /opt/testdata so the fetcher image runs without a mounted checkout.
test/data/**
test/output/**
!test/data/datasets.yaml
!test/data/fetch_test_data.py
!test/data/fetch_test_data.R
!test/data/README.md

# ---- Python ------------------------------------------------------------------
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info
**/.venv
**/venv
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.hypothesis
**/.tox
**/htmlcov
**/.coverage
python/build
python/dist

# ---- R -----------------------------------------------------------------------
**/.Rproj.user
**/.Rhistory
**/.RData
**/*.Rcheck
**/*.tar.gz
r/src/*.o
r/src/*.so
r/inst/doc

# ---- documentation -----------------------------------------------------------
# The images do not serve the docs; CI renders and publishes them separately.
docs/_site
docs/.quarto
docs/reference
docs/r
**/.quarto

# ---- caches and outputs ------------------------------------------------------
# Weights belong in the named cache volume, not a layer. Baking a 2 GB
# SystemsAge tensor into the image would also bake in its licence problem.
.cache
hf_cache
**/*.safetensors
**/*.pt
**/*.pth
# Except the one set that is meant to be in the image: AltumAge is MIT and its
# network is a bundled tier A clock. Excluded here, it built an image whose
# registry declared a weights file the image did not contain.
!python/src/falconage/registry/data/coefficients/*.safetensors
results
results_*
**/*.h5ad
scratch
tmp

# ---- editors and OS ----------------------------------------------------------
.vscode
.idea
**/.DS_Store
**/Thumbs.db
**/desktop.ini
**/*.swp

# ---- the Dockerfiles themselves ---------------------------------------------
# Kept OUT of the context deliberately: an image that contains its own build
# recipe invalidates its own cache every time a comment in that recipe changes.
# install_deps.R and entrypoint.sh are the exceptions -- the image runs them.
docker/Dockerfile.*
docker/docker-compose.yml
